<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Magna Carta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1215">1215</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Magna Carta is the charter of liberties that rebellious barons forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to accept at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/runnymede">Runnymede</a> in June 1215. It limited royal power, protected the rights of the barons and the church, and promised lawful judgement to free men. Annulled by the pope within months, it was reissued in revised forms and became a lasting symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/magna-carta.jpg" alt="Magna Carta" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original authors were the barons and King John of England. Uploaded by Earthsound. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Magna_Carta_(British_Library_Cotton_MS_Augustus_II.106).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Henry II (r. 1154–1189) developed the common law, with itinerant royal justices, standard writs and sworn juries, and in 1215 rebellious barons forced King John to accept <b>Magna Carta</b> at Runnymede, establishing that the king was bound by law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Golden Bull of 1222, issued seven years after England's <b>Magna Carta</b>, allowed the nobles to resist a king who broke its terms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Magna Carta</b> of 1215 was written in Latin, and in the Kingdom of Hungary it remained the language of government until 1844.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The Strömstad Treaty of 1751 between Sweden and Norway had an annex, the Lapp Codicil, sometimes called the “Sámi <b>Magna Carta</b>”, regulating how Sámi used the land across the border.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sami">Sámi · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Great lords limited kings, as the English barons did through <b>Magna Carta</b> in 1215, and in some states nobles ruled outright: the patricians of the Republic of Venice, or the szlachta of the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth, who from 1573 elected their kings.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Medieval kingship in Europe was limited by custom, by the Church and by the estates of the realm: <b>Magna Carta</b> of 1215 bound King John to rule according to law, and the English Parliament, which took shape under Edward I, won the right to consent to taxation.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
